---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Our Consent-First Promise | ScamPrep"
  shareTitle="How we run drills with dignity and caution"
  description="Every drill starts with personal consent and ends with gentle coaching. We never touch bank accounts, and text or phone drills need written consent."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="kicker">Our promise</p>
      <h1>How we run drills with dignity and caution.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Sending a practice scam to someone's mom is a serious thing to do. Here is exactly how
        we handle that responsibility.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight section" style="padding-bottom: 96px;">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div class="promise-item">
        <h2>1. Consent, personally and always.</h2>
        <p>
          We do not run drills for anyone who has not personally and knowingly consented via every channel in which they receive drills. They know drills will come; they just don't know when, and that's what makes practice honest. Enrollment can be withdrawn any time, instantly.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="promise-item">
        <h2>2. Gentle by design.</h2>
        <p>
          Drills never involve real money, real risk, or real consequences. Coaching moments are brief, warm, and private. We never use fear, shame, or “gotcha” language. It’s not the right thing to do, and research is clear that it backfires.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="promise-item">
        <h2>3. Right-fit enrollment.</h2>
        <p>
          ScamPrep is designed for cognitively healthy adults who manage their own email and
          phone. It is not appropriate for people with significant cognitive impairment, and our
          enrollment process screens for fit. If a Resilience Report trend suggests changing needs, we tell them privately and leave future decisions to their discretion.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="promise-item">
        <h2>4. Channels earn their way in.</h2>
        <p>
          We start with email. Text and voice drills are added only with prior express
          <strong>written</strong> consent, per federal telemarketing law (TCPA), requirements we built our process to exceed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="promise-item">
        <h2>5. Data minimalism.</h2>
        <p>
          We collect only what drills require (name, contact channels, drill history). We never access bank accounts, credit reports, or financial data. We never sell personal data.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="promise-item">
        <h2>6. Debrief, always.</h2>
        <p>
          Every missed drill ends in the same place: a clear “this was a safe practice from
          ScamPrep” message and a specific, usable lesson. Nobody is ever left wondering whether
          it was real.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top: 40px;">
        If you're evaluating ScamPrep and want to go deeper on any of this, write us:
        <a href="mailto:hello@getscamprep.com">hello@getscamprep.com</a>. We'll
        answer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand />
</Base>

<style>
  /* Six numbered commitments, separated by hairlines. (No accent rule down
     the left edge: the numbers already do that job.) */
  .promise-item {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-top: 30px;
    margin-bottom: 34px;
  }
  .promise-item:first-child { border-top: 0; padding-top: 0; }
  .promise-item h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: 0; }
  .promise-item p { color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>
